\statement{A.84}{Proved}{The actual compact augmentation module has orientation-twisted projective duality}
Let E be the pure negative pants extension of A.82. For every prime p
put \(\Lambda=\mathbb Z_p[[E]]\) and let
\(\iota:\Lambda\longrightarrow\Lambda\) be the continuous
anti-involution induced by \(g\mapsto g^{-1}\). For a finitely generated
projective compact left \(\Lambda\)-module P define its projective dual by
\[
 P^\#=\operatorname{Hom}_{\Lambda,\mathrm{cont}}(P,\Lambda),
 \qquad (a\cdot f)(v)=f(v)\iota(a).
 \tag{A.84.1}
\]
This is a compact left module; it is not the linear or Pontryagin dual
of the underlying infinite compact abelian group. The actual module of
A.82 satisfies
\[
                         R_p^\#\simeq R_p\otimes\varepsilon.
 \tag{A.84.2}
\]
For every odd p and every finite head \(Q=E/K\), where
\(K\trianglelefteq_o E\) and \(K\subseteq F\), the finite module
\(\overline R=\mathbb F_p\otimes_{\mathbb Z_p}(R_p)_K\)
therefore satisfies the actual isomorphism
\[
                         \overline R^*\simeq
                                    \overline R\otimes\varepsilon,
 \tag{A.84.3}
\]
including when \(p\mid |Q|\).

More precisely, let S be a simple \(\mathbb F_p[Q]\)-module and
\(P(S)\) its projective cover. If \(S\simeq S^*\), its projective
cover occurs as a direct summand of \(\overline R\) exactly
\[
                          2\dim_{\operatorname{End}_Q(S)}S
 \tag{A.84.4}
\]
times. When every simple is self-dual, this gives
\(\overline R\simeq\mathbb F_p[Q]^2\), without the
prime-to-order restriction. In the modular case (A.84.4) concerns
projective-cover summands, not composition-factor multiplicities.
Neither (A.84.2) nor (A.84.4) removes the nonselfdual multiplicity
ambiguity or proves the required incidence map surjective.

\begin{proof}
\emph{The finite projective comparison.}\par
We use the injectivity of the Cartan map for finite group algebras,
in the following precise form: two finite projective modules over a
splitting field of positive characteristic are isomorphic if their
composition-factor multiplicities agree. The proof of
\citet[Proposition~10.2.1, Theorem~10.2.2, and Corollary~10.2.4]{AWebb2016}
was read at this step. Its character pairing identifies the pairing of
a projective-cover character with a simple character as a Kronecker
delta, so projective characters are independent. Additivity on
composition series then gives the asserted comparison. Only
injectivity is used; an integral surjectivity assertion for the
Cartan map is not required.

The comparison also holds over the actual field \(k=\mathbb F_p\).
Choose a finite splitting extension \(k'/k\). Scalar extension
preserves projectivity and composition-factor equality, so the two
extended projectives are isomorphic. On restriction of scalars this
is an isomorphism of d copies of the original modules, where
\(d=[k':k]\). Finite-length Krull--Schmidt cancellation makes their
indecomposable multiplicities equal and hence gives an isomorphism
over k. This is an explicit descent argument, rather than an
assumption that the head is split over \(\mathbb F_p\).

By A.82, \(\overline R\) is projective. Its ordinary dual is
projective as well: the dual of the regular finite group algebra is
isomorphic to its regular module, by the group basis and its dual
basis, and a dual direct summand is again a direct summand.
The orientation twist preserves projectivity. A.83 gives equality
of the composition-factor classes of these two projectives, even
at modular odd heads. The preceding comparison proves (A.84.3).

\emph{Use a dual that commutes with the actual finite base changes.}\par
For \(a\geq1\) and \(K\trianglelefteq_o E\), \(K\subseteq F\), put
\[
 \Lambda_{a,K}=(\mathbb Z/p^a)[E/K],\qquad
 P_{a,K}=\Lambda_{a,K}\widehat\otimes_\Lambda P.
 \tag{A.84.5}
\]
Every such quotient ideal is stable under \(\iota\) and under the
orientation twist. A finite idempotent-matrix description of P shows
that \(P^\#\) is finitely generated projective and gives canonical
base-change identifications
\[
 (P^\#)_{a,K}\simeq(P_{a,K})^\#.
 \tag{A.84.6}
\]
Here the symbol \(\#\) on the right uses definition (A.84.1)
over the finite ring \(\Lambda_{a,K}\).
For a finite free module this is coordinatewise reduction of
the Hom matrix, and a projective module is the image of an idempotent
matrix; reducing that same idempotent proves the assertion for its
direct summand. No flatness of the finite quotient is assumed.

When \(a=1\), the finite group algebra admits the coefficient map
\(t:k[Q]\to k\) taking the coefficient of the identity. The map
\[
 f\longmapsto t\circ f,
 \qquad
 \ell\longmapsto\left(v\longmapsto
                  \sum_{g\in Q}\ell(g^{-1}v)g\right)
 \tag{A.84.7}
\]
gives mutually inverse maps between the right-hand side of
(A.84.6) and the ordinary contragredient dual of \(P_{1,K}\).
For example the coefficient of g in a \(k[Q]\)-linear \(f(v)\)
is \(t(f(g^{-1}v))\). Also
\(t(f(v)g^{-1})=t(f(g^{-1}v))\), so the stated left action is
exactly the contragredient action. Thus the finite duality used in
(A.84.3) is the reduction of the projective dual (A.84.1).

\emph{Lift each finite isomorphism, then make the choices compatible.}\par
Fix an odd p. Write \(M=R_p^\#\) and
\(N=R_p\otimes\varepsilon\). We have just proved
\(M_{1,K}\simeq N_{1,K}\) for every K. Both \(M_{a,K}\) and
\(N_{a,K}\) are finite projective \(\Lambda_{a,K}\)-modules.
Projectivity of the source lifts the composite
\(M_{a,K}\to M_{1,K}\simeq N_{1,K}\) through
\(N_{a,K}\twoheadrightarrow N_{1,K}\). The resulting map
\(M_{a,K}\to N_{a,K}\) is an isomorphism: its cokernel has zero
reduction modulo p and is killed by \(p^a\), hence is zero;
the surjection splits by target projectivity, and the kernel has
the same zero-reduction property and is zero too.

Let \(\mathcal I_{a,K}\) be the finite nonempty set of these
module isomorphisms. The canonical base changes (A.84.6) give
reduction maps between these finite sets. Their compatibility
conditions have the finite intersection property: for any finite
collection of indices and transition conditions, take a common
refinement \((b,L)\), with b at least all the a and L contained
in all the K, choose an isomorphism there, and reduce it to the
specified levels. Compactness of the product of the finite sets
therefore supplies a compatible system. Finite projective modules
over \(\Lambda\) are the inverse limits of their reductions
(A.84.5), as follows from the same idempotent description.
The compatible system and its inverses give (A.84.2).

The quotients with \(K\subseteq F\) are cofinal among the finite
group heads of E, so this is a full completed-group-ring assertion.
We have not taken an inverse limit of ordinary vector-space duals,
whose natural transition maps would point in the opposite direction.
At \(p=2\), (A.84.2) follows directly from A.82: both the projective
dual and the orientation twist of its rank-two free module are
rank-two free.

\emph{Read projective summands, with the correct modular meaning.}\par
For \(A=k[Q]\), the regular module is
\[
 A\simeq\bigoplus_S P(S)^{d_S},\qquad
                         d_S=\dim_{\operatorname{End}_Q(S)}S.
 \tag{A.84.8}
\]
This follows by passing to the semisimple head \(A/\operatorname{rad}A\)
and then taking projective covers. Duality carries \(P(S)\) to
\(P(S^*)\). To check the latter without a semisimplicity assumption,
write \(P(S)=Ae\) for a primitive idempotent e. The group-algebra
coefficient pairing identifies its contragredient dual with
\(A\iota(e)\). The head of this last indecomposable projective is
\(S^*\): the action of \(\iota(e)\) on \(S^*\) is the dual of the
action of e on S, and its action on another simple T vanishes unless
\(T^*\simeq S\). Twisting by \(\varepsilon\) similarly carries
\(P(S)\) to \(P(S\otimes\varepsilon)\).

Let \(n_S\) be the multiplicity of \(P(S)\) in \(\overline R\).
Equation (A.84.3) and the reduction of A.82 give
\[
 n_{S^*}=n_{S\otimes\varepsilon},\qquad
                  n_S+n_{S\otimes\varepsilon}=4d_S.
 \tag{A.84.9}
\]
For self-dual S these imply \(n_S=2d_S\), proving (A.84.4).
If all simples are self-dual, (A.84.8) gives the asserted freeness
at that finite head. For a nonselfdual pair, (A.84.9) still permits
opposite imbalances. We have constructed no such imbalance for the
actual module. The conclusion remains a module theorem: it does not
choose generators that are differences of peripheral vertices and
does not solve the marked generation or full conjugacy questions.
\end{proof}
